 unreached branch arms or alignment padding","restsOn":"a compiled function is one contiguous run, so the few bytes inside it the walk never reached are still its own; a table of entry stubs `movs rX, #k; b T` that share one tail gives each entry its own constant, so an unreached copy of the entry stub is another entry, not dead code","summary":"a dense function's free bytes inside its extent are claimed as dead code, except unreached copies of its own `movs rX, #k; b T` entry stub"},{"adds":["claims"],"id":"region.padding","reads":["rom","claims","function-set"],"recovers":"alignment zeros emitted as data slices between functions instead of .align","restsOn":"agbcc and as emit .align 2, 0 before every function and literal pool, so the only free bytes between code are the zeros of an alignment","summary":"free padding bytes that align the claimed item after them, between claimed bytes below the code end, are claimed as padding"},{"adds":["candidates"],"id":"seed.call","origin":"call","reads":["readings","bl-verdicts"],"recovers":"the call graph below every accepted function","restsOn":"a bl (or a bx after mov lr, pc) that settles as a call enters a function","summary":"the targets of the calls of an accepted function are functions"}]}
